CLK_TCK=100
Field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat is starttime in clock ticks, so its resolution
is 1/CLK_TCK s. This measures how many concurrently-forked processes share a
starttime, which is the precondition for a (pid, starttime) alias.

--- 40 processes forked in one tight loop (each must outlive the read) ---
samples=40 distinct_pids=40 distinct_starttimes=2

starttime histogram (count, tick index):
     36 1294535
      4 1294534

distinct (pid,starttime) pairs = 40 by construction, so no
alias occurred in this sample: the alias needs a *recycled* pid inside one tick.

Interpretation: distinct_starttimes << samples means 1/CLK_TCK s is far coarser
than fork latency, so 'same starttime' is common. A (pid, starttime) ALIAS also
needs the pid to be recycled inside that same tick; forcing that needs
kernel.pid_max wrapping, i.e. privilege this experiment does not have. The alias
itself is therefore tested SYNTHETICALLY in the pidreuse step; its precondition
is measured here.
